//! Allocation: the `Allocator` trait, `GlobalAllocator`, and `AllocError`.
//!
//! ## What happens when the allocator says no
//!
//! Every allocating operation in the standard library answers that question
//! one of two ways, and the name tells you which.
//!
//! An operation with an infallible signature — `Vector.push`, `Box.make`,
//! `StringBuilder.append`, `Map.insert`, a constructor — **panics** on
//! allocation failure. It never truncates, never returns a plausible
//! substitute value, and never hands back an object that has quietly stopped
//! working. In the freestanding profile the panic reaches `__saw_rt_panic`,
//! so a kernel picks the policy (oops, kill the task, reboot).
//!
//! Every such operation has a `try_`-prefixed twin that returns
//! `Result<_, AllocError>` instead: `try_push`, `try_make`,
//! `try_with_capacity`, `try_insert`, `try_append`. That is the surface for
//! code that handles exhaustion locally rather than dying of it, and it is
//! the surface a freestanding or kernel caller is expected to use. `try_`
//! never partially applies its operation — on `Err` the container is exactly
//! as it was.
//!
//! ```saw
//! var frames = Vector<Frame, FrameSlab>()
//! match frames.try_push(Frame(id: 1)) {
//!     case Ok(_) -> print("queued"),
//!     case Err(e) -> print("out of frame memory: {e}")
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! A type parameterized by its allocator (`Vector<T, A>`, `Box<T, A>`,
//! `Map<K, V, A>`, `Set<T, A>`) is the freestanding toolkit, and the `try_`
//! tier is its primary surface. Types with no allocator parameter — `String`,
//! `StringBuilder`, `Data`, `Arc`, `Mutex`, `Channel` — allocate through
//! `GlobalAllocator`.

// Allocator trait, GlobalAllocator allocator, and AllocError
// Part of the Saw standard library — design 19 §4, brief 28 (stage 3)
//
// This is the alloc-layer allocation surface. Alloc-layer containers (Vector,
// Map, Arc, ...) route every allocation through the `Allocator` trait rather
// than calling the `__saw_rt_alloc`/`__saw_rt_dealloc` seams directly. As of stage 4
// (brief 37) the allocator is a PUBLIC type parameter with a default —
// `Vector<T, A: Allocator = GlobalAllocator>`, `Map<K, V, A = GlobalAllocator>` — so hosted code
// omits it (`Vector<T>` fills `A = GlobalAllocator`) while a custom allocator is a
// distinct type end to end. `GlobalAllocator` remains the default and the seam wrapper.
// Still future work: per-type slab allocators (F3) and module-level `static`
// declarations (F4). The core/alloc/std layer split (design 19 §1) is also
// future work — this file builds no layer enforcement.

// =============================================================================
// The global allocation seams (design 20)
// =============================================================================

// Hosted defaults wrap malloc/free; the freestanding profile leaves these as
// declarations the user's environment provides at link time. Because all std
// files are merged into one namespace, these declarations are shared with the
// alloc-layer container modules — they no longer declare the seams themselves.
extern "C" {
    func __saw_rt_alloc(size: Int, align: Int) -> UnsafePointer<Int8>?
    func __saw_rt_dealloc(ptr: UnsafePointer<Int8>, size: Int, align: Int)
}

// =============================================================================
// Allocator trait (design 19 §4)
// =============================================================================

/// The allocation contract every container routes through.
///
/// An implementation reports exhaustion by returning `None` from `alloc`; what
/// happens next is the caller's tier, not the allocator's (see the module
/// documentation). `dealloc` must be handed the same `size`/`align` the block
/// was allocated with: the seams carry no per-block metadata, so the container
/// remembers the layout.
/// Both requirements are `unsafe` (design 130): each names a raw pointer in its
/// signature, so every implementation and every caller is unsafe by the trigger
/// rule. `dealloc` is the shape rule 7 describes — its precondition (the pointer
/// really came from this allocator, with this layout) is expressed by taking an
/// unsafe-typed parameter, which is what carries the obligation to the caller.
public trait Allocator {
    func alloc(&self, size: Int, align: Int) unsafe -> UnsafePointer<Int8>?
    func dealloc(&self, ptr: UnsafePointer<Int8>, size: Int, align: Int) unsafe
}

// =============================================================================
// GlobalAllocator — the default, seam-backed allocator
// =============================================================================

// A zero-field unit struct: it carries no state (its "storage" is whatever the
// __saw_rt_alloc seam is bound to at link time). Being zero-sized, `GlobalAllocator()` is a
// zero-size placeholder and `GlobalAllocator().alloc(...)` monomorphizes to a direct
// call into the seam with no allocator value materialized at runtime — the
// zero-cost static dispatch that design 19 option C relies on.
public struct GlobalAllocator {}

extension GlobalAllocator: Allocator {
    public func alloc(&self, size: Int, align: Int) unsafe -> UnsafePointer<Int8>? {
        __saw_rt_alloc(size, align)
    }

    public func dealloc(&self, ptr: UnsafePointer<Int8>, size: Int, align: Int) unsafe {
        __saw_rt_dealloc(ptr, size, align)
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// AllocError — failure context for fallible factories (design 19 open-Q tier 2)
// =============================================================================

/// The error every `try_` operation reports: the allocator refused a request.
///
/// `size` is the byte count that was asked for and `align` its alignment, so an
/// out-of-memory site can be logged with enough context to tell a 24-byte
/// control block apart from a doubling buffer. It conforms to `Error`, so it
/// boxes into a `Result<T, Box<any Error>>` boundary like any other error, and
/// it is `Printable`.
public struct AllocError {
    /// Byte count of the request that failed.
    public size: Int
    /// Alignment of the request that failed.
    public align: Int
}

extension AllocError: Error {
    public func format(&self, into: &var StringBuilder) {
        into.append("allocation of ")
        into.append(self.size)
        into.append(" bytes (align ")
        into.append(self.align)
        into.append(") failed")
    }
}
